## 0. Executive summary

**Decision: approved with conditions, for rank-ordering in benign conditions only. Not approved for absolute PD, provisioning or capital use.**

Both models rank borrowers well (hold-out AUC {auc_ho_s:.2f} scorecard, {auc_ho_l:.2f} LightGBM). Out of time they keep ranking reasonably (AUC about {auc_cr_s:.2f}), but their predicted default rates are far too low.

Top three findings:
1. **Severe under-prediction in the crisis (F1, High).** For 2006-08 originations the models predict {pred_cr_s:.1f}-{pred_cr_l:.1f}% against {obs_cr:.1f}% observed ({ratio_cr_s:.1f}x scorecard, {ratio_cr_l:.1f}x LightGBM). All {bands_under_cr} of {bands_total} score bands are under-predicted. In 2005 the gap is already {ratio_oot_s:.1f}x and {ratio_oot_l:.1f}x.
2. **The error is concentrated (F2, High).** California loans are under-predicted {seg_state_CA:.0f}x, Nevada {seg_state_NV:.0f}x, Arizona {seg_state_AZ:.0f}x and Florida {seg_state_FL:.0f}x, and broker-channel loans {seg_channel_B:.0f}x. One margin of conservatism would be too much for some segments and too little for others.
3. **Input drift (F3, High).** Interest rate PSI is {psi_int_rate_cr:.1f} in the crisis and {psi_int_rate_rec:.1f} in 2016-19, and the scorecard's own score distribution shifts sharply in 2016-19 (PSI {psi_pd_scorecard_rec:.2f}). In that period the scorecard under-predicts {ratio_rec_s:.1f}x and LightGBM {ratio_rec_l:.2f}x.

Conditions: (a) use only to rank or tier, not to estimate PD; (b) apply a conservatism margin or recalibrate on stressed data before any PD use; (c) monitor PSI and calibration quarterly (Section 10); (d) redevelop if the triggers in Section 10 fire.

