## 7. Sensitivity and stress

Input shocks applied to hold-out and crisis loans (hold-out / crisis):

{tbl_stress}

- The scorecard moves the right way for every shock on every loan, by construction of its monotone binning.
- LightGBM is right on average but **non-monotone for individual loans**: a higher interest rate lowers PD for {wrong_rate_l_ho:.0f}% of hold-out and {wrong_rate_l_cr:.0f}% of crisis loans, and a better credit score raises PD for {wrong_fico_up_l_ho:.0f}%. This would not pass a conduct or adverse-action review.
- Neither model is stress-sensitive in the sense that matters. Shocks to borrower attributes move PD by tens of percent, nowhere near the {badx_cr:.1f}x rise in actual crisis defaults, because the crisis was a macro shock the models cannot see.

