## 8. Explainability

![SHAP importance](figures/shap_importance.png)
![Partial dependence](figures/partial_dependence.png)

- **LightGBM.** SHAP ranks {shap1_name} first ({shap1:.2f}), then {shap2_name} ({shap2:.2f}), {shap3_name} ({shap3:.2f}), {shap4_name} ({shap4:.2f}) and {shap5_name} ({shap5:.2f}). Partial dependence for credit score, LTV and DTI is smooth and in the expected direction. Number of borrowers has only two values, so its curve is a straight line.
- **Scorecard.** Each loan gets reason codes from points lost against the best bin. Across hold-out loans the most frequent top-three reason is {rc_top1} ({rc_top1_v:.0f}% of reasons), followed by credit score ({rc_fico:.0f}%), LTV ({rc_ltv:.0f}%) and number of borrowers ({rc_n_borrowers:.0f}%). These can support adverse-action explanations. That state tops the list is a concern (Section 9).
- **Agreement.** The two models' PDs correlate at {rho_score:.2f} (Spearman), but their feature rankings agree only moderately ({rho_driver:.2f}).

Overall the scorecard is the more explainable and defensible model.

